      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could the body say what fails without this patch? As far as I can see, `mlock()` never mlocks a large file folio in a 4KB VMA: `allow_mlock_munlock()` compares the PTE count from `folio_mlock_step()` with `folio_nr_pages()`, and `folio_within_range()` mixes 16KB file pages with 4KB VMA pages. Faults don\u0027t either: since upstream ab521b4142aa (\"mm/rmap: improve mlock tracking for large folios\"), `__folio_add_file_rmap()` mlocks a large folio when one call maps all of it, but `finish_fault()` maps one slice per fault in a 4KB VMA and `should_fault_around()` is off. So the folio stays evictable until reclaim restores the mlock, which `mlock_file_slices_p3s` (aosp/4410080) checks through /proc/kpageflags.\n\nThat would also explain `p3s_mlock_file_folio()`: it gives 4KB VMAs the fault-time mlock that native VMAs already get. Something like:\n\n```\nmlock() and MLOCK_ONFAULT leave large file folios in 4KB VMAs\nevictable until reclaim restores the mlock. mlock_pte_range() never\nmlocks them: allow_mlock_munlock() compares a count of 4KB PTEs with\nfolio_nr_pages(), and folio_within_range() mixes 16KB file pages with\n4KB VMA pages. Faults don\u0027t either: __folio_add_file_rmap() only\nmlocks a large folio that one call maps completely, and finish_fault()\nmaps one slice per fault in a 4KB VMA.\n\nCount the consecutive slice PTEs that map the folio under the page\ntable lock instead, and mlock the folio once all of its slices are\nmapped, both in mlock_pte_range() and when a fault maps the last\nmissing slice. Native VMAs are unaffected.\n```\n\nNit: as on aosp/4409398, could you wrap the body at 72 columns and drop the blank line between `Bug:` and `Change-Id:`?",
